      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's an AI-generated summary of page 2 of an OIML Certificate No R76/2006-GB1-12.10. The fragment contains a conversational summary, not the actual certificate markdown.\n\nWhat can I extract?\n\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-GB1-12.10. Page 2 of 3, so page_total: 3.\n- Recommendation: R76 (from \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" — the scheme R76, 2006 edition). Actually the number format is R76/2006-GB1-12.10 where R76 is the recommendation, 2006 is edition.\n- Evaluation Report P00917 (13 pages) — test_reports entry with role type_evaluation_report.\n- Certified type: non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators with model designations: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS. Category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — well, the category list in the schema mentions \"Indicator\", etc. The certified type is a weighing instrument family... Actually wait — this is an R76 certificate for... hmm. The description says \"a family of non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators (AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS models) connected to a weighing platform\". So the certified type is digital indicators (A-Series and V-Series) that are certified for use in non-automatic weighing instruments. Category could be \"Indicator\" since the models are indicators. The models: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS (A-Series); VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS (V-Series).\n\n- Accuracy class: Class III or IIII.\n- Power: mains or battery.\n- Enclosure: stainless steel (AFS, APS, VFS, VPS), plastic (AFM, ATM, ABM, VFM, VBM, VTM).\n- Displays: 2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD depending on model.\n- Load cell connections: 4-wire (A-Series) or 6-wire (V-Series).\n\nModel-level characteristics: enclosure material, display size, load cell wiring differ per model. This is a model family with 10 models.\n\nShould I create model_level entries? Yes — enclosure_material varies by model: stainless steel for AFS, APS, VFS, VPS; plastic for AFM, ATM, ABM, VFM, VBM, VTM. Load cell connection wires: 4 (A-Series), 6 (V-Series). Display size: 2.0\" or 1.0\" depending on model — but we don't know which model has which, so maybe record as type_level with value... Actually \"2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD displays depending on model\" — since the mapping is unknown, I could record display_size at model_level with values only where known — but it's not known. Better to record at type_level as a string \"2.0 in or 1.0 in\"? Hmm. The rule says don't fabricate. I could include a characteristic `display` with value \"graphic LCD\" and note 2.0\" or 1.0\". Maybe safer: type_level characteristic \"display_type\": \"graphic LCD\", and maybe omit size or record as config? I'll record display_diagonal at type_level as a string list? The rules say lists of values across models become model matrix; but here we don't know the mapping. I could record display_diagonal_inches with value {min:1.0, max:2.0}? That's a bit of a stretch. Maybe just record \"graphic LCD\" type and mention \"1.0 in or 2.0 in\" as a string value. Since it says \"2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD displays depending on model\", I can do model_level with... no mapping. I'll put type_level `display: {value: \"2.0 in or 1.0 in graphic LCD\"}`? Hmm, structured values require value object with unit. For strings, value can be a string. Let me do:\n\n- display_type: {value: \"graphic LCD\", unit_symbol: null}\n- display_diagonal: {value: {min: 1.0, max: 2.0}, unit_symbol: \"in\"}? That misrepresents—it's two discrete values, not a range. Hmm. Could use model_level with per-model values? Not known. I'll keep as a string value: {value: \"1.0 or 2.0\", unit_symbol: \"in\"}? Strings allowed when formula/symbol. Lists though... I think it's acceptable to record as string since the mapping to models is missing. Or just skip the sizes and note in description. The description field can hold the free-text description.\n\nAccuracy class: III or IIII → recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"].\n\nScheme: The cert number R76/2006-GB1-12.10 — for R76, the scheme... R76 has OIML CS scheme? The certificate number format \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" is the older format, not the OIML-CS A/B scheme format. Scheme field: schema says scheme (A|B). We don't have explicit info; but OIML CS certificates since 2017 have format like \"R76/2020-GB1-...\". Actually \"GB1\" indicates the issuing authority (UK? GB = Great Britain, NWML?). Hmm, \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" — this is the classic OIML certificate numbering used before OIML-CS. I should be cautious: scheme A or B — not stated. I'll omit scheme or... The recommendation section has scheme too. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Omit.\n\ncertificate.number: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\". page_total: 3. project_number? Not present. member_state? Not present.\n\ntest_reports: id \"P00917\", pages 13, role \"type_evaluation_report\" (Evaluation Report = type evaluation report). Date not present.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"? The models are indicators A-Series/V-Series. The certificate covers digital indicators for use in non-automatic weighing instruments. Given the fragment says \"a family of non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators\", the certified type is the indicators. Category: \"Indicator\". Hmm. Let me think: \"A family of non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators (AFS, ... models) connected to a weighing platform\" — the indicators are certified as instruments. Typically these NWL indicator certificates say \"Digital indicator ... for use in non-automatic weighing instruments\". Category \"Indicator\" seems right.\n\n- type_designations: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS. Also series names A-Series, V-Series.\n\nmodel_family: family_name: null; models with model_id each. Attributes per model: series (A or V), enclosure_material, load_cell_connection_wires.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- enclosure_material: values per model: \"stainless steel\" for AFS, APS, VFS, VPS; \"plastic\" for AFM, ATM, ABM, VFM, VBM, VTM.\n- load_cell_connection: 4-wire for A-Series models (AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS), 6-wire for V-Series (VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS).\n\nAccuracy class at type_level: accuracy_class: {value: \"III or IIII\"} — string value fine.\n\nPower supply: mains or battery → power_supply: {value: \"mains or battery\"}. Could split? \"mains or battery-powered\" — keep string.\n\nComponents: \"A/D conversion module, main board with processor\" — these are internal components, maybe not worth listing as components since they're sub-assemblies inside the indicator. Rule 6: components for complex instruments composed of modules. The A/D conversion module and main board are part of the indicator; could include as components with role. I might include them:\n- {role: \"A/D conversion module\", type_designations: []}\n- {role: \"Main board with processor\"}\nHmm, type_designations empty — could omit type_designations. I think including them is optional; I'll include briefly. Actually rule 8: don't fabricate; they are named sub-assemblies. I'll include.\n\nFunctions/devices: zero setting/tracking, subtractive tare, etc. Could record as characteristics type_level strings: functions. Maybe a single characteristic \"devices\": {value: \"zero setting/tracking, subtractive tare balancing, stability of equilibrium determination, zero and net indicators, memory/totalisation, printing\"}. That's reasonable.\n\nLoad cell requirements: any compatible load cell with R60 OIML CoC; NH-marked cells only allowed with R76 humidity testing. This is a condition — maybe record as config_level? Axis: load cell humidity classification (NH vs others). Condition \"NH-marked load cell\" → value \"allowed only with R76 humidity testing\". Hmm, this could be config_level:\n- attribute: \"load_cell_acceptance\", axis: \"load cell marking\", values: [{condition: \"NH-marked load cell\", value: \"only with R76 humidity testing of the instrument\"}]. That seems overreach. Alternatively include in description text. I'll include description capturing it, plus maybe a components note that load cells are external with requirement. Actually components: load cell is a module connected. Could add component {role: \"load cell\", ...alternatives \"any compatible load cell with R60 OIML certificate\"}. I'll add:\n{role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics?}. Schema for components: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can put characteristics: {\"requirement\": {value: \"R60 OIML Certificate of Conformity\"}}. Hmm, characteristics{} in components — shape unspecified; I'll keep it simple with a description-ish. Maybe better to avoid overcomplicating. I'll include a component entry for load cell with a note.\n\nFootnotes: none present.\n\nRevision history: none.\n\nDate issued: not present in fragment. Issuing authority: not present (GB1 hints UK but not stated). Don't fabricate.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R76\", edition 2006. The cert number \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" — edition 2006 (R76-1 2006 edition). accuracy_classes [\"III\",\"IIII\"].\n\nNow build JSON.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. enclosure_material — unit null, values string.\n2. load_cell_connection — attribute \"load_cell_connection_wiring\", values \"4-wire\"/\"6-wire\". Or numeric wires count with unit \"wires\"? value 4, unit \"wire\". I'll do attribute load_cell_connection, value \"4-wire\"/\"6-wire\" strings. Simpler.\n3. series: A-Series vs V-Series per model.\n\nModels list: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS. Each with attributes: series, enclosure_material, load_cell_connection.\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III or IIII\"} — value as string \"III or IIII\". Hmm structured: {\"value\": \"III or IIII\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}.\n- power_supply: {value: \"mains or battery\"}\n- display_type: {value: \"graphic LCD\"}\n- display_diagonal: maybe {\"value\": \"1.0 or 2.0\", \"unit_symbol\": \"in\"}? I'd rather include as string. Let me include: {\"attribute\": \"display_diagonal\", value \"1.0 or 2.0\", unit \"in\"}. Eh. Alternatively skip and put in description. I'll include as string value since mapping unknown.\n\nAlso \"self-indicating\", \"single or dual interval/range\" → characteristics: weighing_capability: \"single or dual interval/range\"; indication: \"self-indicating\". These are strings. Maybe include \"scale_intervals\": {value: \"single or dual interval/range\"}. Keep a couple.\n\nDevices functions string.\n\nConfig-level: NH-marked load cells condition. I'll add:\nconfig_level entry: attribute \"nh_marked_load_cell_usage\", axis \"load cell humidity classification\", values: [{condition: \"NH-marked load cell\", value: \"permitted only if instrument passed R76 humidity test\"}]. Hmm, value shape: config values follow structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. value string here. I think it's OK but slightly fabricated in wording. Use close-to-source wording: \"only allowed with R76 humidity testing\". Good.\n\nmatrix_tables: none.\n\nDescription: \"Family of non-automatic weighing instruments (digital indicators) ... suitable for direct sales to the public.\"\n\nAlso components: A/D conversion module; main board with processor; weighing and load cell connections. I'll add:\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"display\", characteristics: {...}}? Overkill. Keep: \n- {role: \"A/D conversion module\"}\n- {role: \"main board with processor\"}\n- {role: \"load cell\", alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {requirement: \"R60 OIML Certificate of Conformity; compatibility of modules data required; NH-marked cells only with R76 humidity testing; standard-type transmission\"}}\n\nHmm characteristics{} free-form. Values should be structured? Rule 2 says every